ero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" should be an alias of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prince Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", NOT \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the mummer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n    61→The LLM is making the wrong merge decision because:\\\\\\\\n    62→- Both \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the mummer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" appear in the climactic confrontation scene\\\\\\\\n    63→- The LLM may be confused by the masquerade setting (costumes, mummers)\\\\\\\\n    64→- The context doesn't clearly distinguish that Prospero is the HUMAN and the mummer is DEATH\\\\\\\\n    65→\\\\\\\\n    66→## Current Issues (Priority Order)\\\\\\\\n    67→\\\\\\\\n    68→### CRITICAL\\\\\\\\n    69→1. **False character merge: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" merged with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the mummer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" instead of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prince Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"**\\\\\\\\n    70→   - Problem: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (short for Prince Prospero) is incorrectly listed as an alias of \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the mummer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (the Red Death)\\\\\\\\n    71→   - Evidence: The text clearly shows Prince Prospero is KILLED BY the mummer: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fell prostrate in death the Prince Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    72→   - Root Cause: The pairwise merge decision is comparing:\\\\\\\\n    73→     - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (4 mentions, standalone uses)\\\\\\\\n    74→     - \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the mummer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (3 mentions)\\\\\\\\n    75→     - And deciding they're the same person (WRONG)\\\\\\\\n    76→   - Meanwhile \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prince Prospero\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (3 mentions) is kept separate\\\\\\\\n    77→   - Impact: Protagonist and antagonist are merged (-4 points to character score)\\\\\\\\n    78→\\\\\\\\n    79→2. **POST-PROCESSING fix didn't trigger**\\\\\\\\n    80→   - The `_split_on_death_evidence()` function exists but didn't split the characters\\\\\\\\n    81→   - Likely because: mention contexts don't include the death sce